@*
    A task's detail pane: the groups of rows that say when it happens, how it
    ranks, and what hangs off it.

    Two columns, because these rows are short and the panel they sit in is not.
    Every row in one stack means reading past the whole of "when" to find out how
    important the thing is, and it pushes the body — the part somebody opened the
    panel to write — below the fold on a laptop. Side by side, the pane is one
    glance.

    The columns balance rather than being placed, which is the decision worth
    arguing here. A grid puts a third group under the first one and leaves a hole
    beside it, so a pane of one tall group and two short ones comes out a row
    taller than anything in it. Columns fill instead: the short groups stack under
    one another in the second column, and the pane is as tall as its longest
    column. That is vertical space the body gets back on every task.

    How many columns there are is the width's answer and not a number written down
    here, so the same pane is a single column when there is no room for a second
    one. That is a requirement rather than a nicety: the mobile channel is a single
    column by design, and a pane that insisted on two would give it two unreadable
    ones. The column width is also what stops a third one appearing inside a panel
    of the width this is drawn at — three subjects abreast would be a row of
    captions, not a pane.

    Above the columns there is room for one row on its own, across the full width.
    That is not a slot for anything that would not fit: it is for the row that is a
    decision rather than a fact — "put this in today" — and it is one row because
    two things at the top of a pane is a pane with nothing at the top of it.

    Nothing here says which groups a pane has, or what goes in the lead. What a
    task can be set to is the host's answer, the same way it is for every other
    component in this folder.
*@

<div class="@ClassValue" data-testid="@TestId">
    @if (Lead is not null)
    {
        @* Above the columns rather than in them, so it spans the pane whatever the
           width has decided the columns are. *@
        <div class="task-action-pane__lead">@Lead</div>
    }

    @* The columns belong to this box and not to the pane itself, because the lead
       row must not be in them: a row that flowed with the groups would be balanced
       along with them and end up as the top of one column rather than across both. *@
    <div class="task-action-pane__columns">@Groups</div>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>The one row that goes across the top, when there is one. Left out
    /// entirely when there is not, so a pane without one starts at its first
    /// group.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? Lead { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The groups, in reading order. <see cref="TaskActionGroup"/> in
    /// practice: a pane of ungrouped rows would be the stack this exists to stop
    /// being.
    /// <para>
    /// Named rather than taken as child content, which it has to be once there are
    /// two slots — and which is the better read anyway: a pane declares a lead and
    /// its groups, rather than a lead and whatever else was lying around.
    /// </para></summary>
    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? Groups { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? CssClass { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? TestId { get; set; }

    private string ClassValue =>
        string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(CssClass) ? "task-action-pane" : $"task-action-pane {CssClass}";
}
